WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.470
(bass and drums)

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.273
- Hey, I'm Adam.

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:09.410
I come from a jazz and rock background.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.243
- I'm Nahre.

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:12.207
I come from a classical piano background.

00:00:12.207 --> 00:00:14.022
- And I'm LA and I come from a gospel

00:00:14.022 --> 00:00:16.100
and hip-hop, R&amp;B background.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.550
- [Nahre] And we're gonna chat
about some of our thoughts

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.370
surrounding learning by ear

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.880
versus learning with sheet music.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.590
- And then we're gonna jam
- Yes

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.330
- and put all this to test.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:27.320
- It's gonna be dope.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:28.250
- Yes,
(laughs)

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:29.296
agreed.

00:00:29.296 --> 00:00:30.949
(electronic jazz)

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:33.699
- Whoo, play it, Nahre, go ahead!

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.410
- Before I started lessons,

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:48.830
I just really liked the keyboard

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:52.030
and then I copied what
my teacher was doing,

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.870
but you can only keep that
up to a certain point.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.900
If you don't know how to
read, you're outta luck.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.733
(electronic jazz)

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.346
- I would play to records in my basement,

00:01:09.346 --> 00:01:12.480
playing to Beyonce, a
concert, watching the DVDs.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.313
- Nice.

00:01:13.313 --> 00:01:14.146
That's how I learned,
you know what I'm sayin',

00:01:14.146 --> 00:01:14.979
arrangements.

00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:15.812
- Watching.

00:01:15.812 --> 00:01:18.868
Watching is just as important
as a high percent of playing.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:21.701
(electronic jazz)

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.950
- I started by learning piano

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.800
and then I learned how to play bass

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.330
because there was a band starting

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:35.307
and they just needed a bass player,

00:01:35.307 --> 00:01:38.274
and I promptly stopped playing piano

00:01:38.274 --> 00:01:40.300
and I said, like, this is the cool thing,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.870
yeah, I'm gonna learn.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.223
- Sorry, Nahre.

00:01:43.223 --> 00:01:44.056
(laughs)

00:01:44.056 --> 00:01:46.889
(electronic jazz)

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.333
- [LA] Yeah.

00:01:58.333 --> 00:01:59.166
- [Adam] That was it.
- Cool.

00:01:59.166 --> 00:02:00.050
- That was a tune, that was a tune.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:01.700
- [LA] Now are you, wait,
you don't know how to jam?

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:02.533
That's perfect.

00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:03.576
- I've never jammed like this before.

00:02:03.576 --> 00:02:05.610
Well, this is such a relief

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:08.530
because we've always been communicating

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.640
via video talk and everything

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.137
but this is,

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:13.970
this is where--
- In person.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:15.210
- Making music together.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.470
- You guys are playing, yeah.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.303
The first time you guys get to play.

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.207
- Hit together at the same time, yeah.

00:02:18.207 --> 00:02:21.392
In gospel music, as far as
the energy is concerned,

00:02:21.392 --> 00:02:23.640
a level one is, like, the basic groove.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.319
Level two is the bridge,
it's the next level energy,

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:27.000
and level three is the vamp.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.440
That's where all the craziness happens,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.087
all the extra chords, hits,
lines, all that, level three,

00:02:32.087 --> 00:02:33.553
and you gotta end on level three.

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:35.346
(bass and drums)
All right.

00:02:35.346 --> 00:02:36.969
This'll be a good thing
to do them solos on.

00:02:36.969 --> 00:02:39.390
Level one, level two, level three.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:40.393
Try that.

00:02:41.618 --> 00:02:42.451
It's on you.

00:02:42.451 --> 00:02:43.284
- Level one?

00:02:43.284 --> 00:02:44.117
- [LA] It's on you.

00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:45.367
It's level one.

00:02:46.622 --> 00:02:49.205
(piano begins)

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.680
- One thing I realize about,
you know, in this setting,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.770
is we have to be sensitive

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:01.673
and honorable.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.330
Level two right here.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:06.163
Three, four.

00:03:06.163 --> 00:03:08.492
(slow jazz)

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:14.210
- Sometimes this sort of situation's hard

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:17.300
because you're so used to
wanting music to be good

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.501
or right and this exercise is
much more about opening up.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.242
(slow jazz)

00:03:23.242 --> 00:03:24.075
- [LA] We're going to level three.

00:03:24.075 --> 00:03:26.237
You got to go level three
so really let it all out.

00:03:26.237 --> 00:03:29.237
(intense fast jazz)

00:03:50.808 --> 00:03:54.050
- It does take awhile to
get into that mindset,

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:55.592
especially if you've never done it before,

00:03:55.592 --> 00:03:57.710
which is crazy, Nahre,

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:59.460
that this was your first time doing this.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.356
- Yeah.
- It was magic.

00:04:00.356 --> 00:04:01.228
- We just, like, went nuts.

00:04:01.228 --> 00:04:04.300
- I'm used to reading
cues in a different way.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.525
In classical music there's
usually a designated person,

00:04:07.525 --> 00:04:10.490
in a string quartet it'll
be the first violinist,

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.013
and everyone will pay attention to them.

00:04:13.013 --> 00:04:15.846
(slow funky jazz)

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.070
- One skill that I
learned, so just in church,

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:28.120
that isn't taught in school
is the skill of lip reading,

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.180
being able to play while
you're still reading

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.490
and understanding and then
process that information

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.014
and translate it over to your instrument.

00:04:35.014 --> 00:04:37.431
(funky jazz)

00:04:44.886 --> 00:04:47.219
Pull out the bridge, my man.

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:49.597
Keep goin', keep goin'.

00:04:49.597 --> 00:04:50.922
(intense drums)

00:04:50.922 --> 00:04:51.755
Okay.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.140
- [Nahre] That helps, level
one, level two, level three.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.780
- I played a gig with
the Minnesota orchestra.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:14.990
So it was at this church,

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:16.650
we were doin' this gospel concert thing,

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.770
and I could not watch the conductor.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.003
For my world,

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.020
this,
- That's the downbeat.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.720
As soon as, this is the ichthus,

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.920
as soon I get down there,

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.530
that's when the note should come out.

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:28.893
So it's like, buh.

00:05:30.577 --> 00:05:31.833
Classical is, uhn.

00:05:31.833 --> 00:05:32.700
(laughs)

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.480
- There's apparently a
good reason for that.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.660
It's for the conductor to telegraph

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:37.757
what it's supposed to be.

00:05:37.757 --> 00:05:41.519
- Especially because every
conductor is different.

00:05:41.519 --> 00:05:42.590
- Yeah.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:46.680
- Some are very ahead of the
beat and some are very exact.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.270
- So it's like once you learn
the language of following

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.560
the conductor you have
to fine tune the language

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.360
to the specific conductor
that you're, okay.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:55.680
That's really hard to do.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:56.770
I had to watch the violinists.

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:58.900
I had to watch their bows for the rhythm.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:00.070
I was close to them.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:01.940
I didn't know what else
to pay attention to.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.357
(slow music)

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.160
So to me, in church you're going,

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:24.725
are we going to the next part?

00:06:24.725 --> 00:06:25.558
That's all I need.

00:06:25.558 --> 00:06:27.151
- Yeah, but that to me is amazing.

00:06:27.151 --> 00:06:29.970
How can you follow all those cues,

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:34.110
know what's going on based on the context

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:37.840
and retain all of that
information musically

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.813
and be able to improvise over it?

00:06:40.813 --> 00:06:43.700
- Hey, Nahre, find something
cool that you wrote,

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.001
your thing, find a cool
chunk that we can jam on.

00:06:46.001 --> 00:06:48.418
(funky jazz)

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.000
I'll find you wherever you go.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:53.833
I'll find you.

00:06:57.043 --> 00:06:57.876
Yeah.

00:06:57.876 --> 00:06:58.709
- Yeah.

00:07:00.735 --> 00:07:01.568
- [LA] Wow.

00:07:01.568 --> 00:07:03.235
- [Adam] This is it.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.110
I tell my students, the
more you move your body,

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.416
the better your time will be.

00:07:08.416 --> 00:07:09.249
- Yeah.

00:07:09.249 --> 00:07:11.666
(funky jazz)

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:15.433
- [LA] Yeah, yeah!

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:28.410
- For the longest time I didn't
get the music in my body.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:31.817
- That's kind of where I struggle.

00:07:31.817 --> 00:07:34.234
(funky jazz)

00:07:35.741 --> 00:07:36.654
- Yeah, just try it again, try it again.

00:07:36.654 --> 00:07:38.069
Try it again, Nahre.

00:07:38.069 --> 00:07:39.152
Try it again.

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:41.524
Find it there, find it there.

00:07:41.524 --> 00:07:42.695
You got it.

00:07:42.695 --> 00:07:44.816
Do it again, do it again.

00:07:44.816 --> 00:07:47.970
- I visualize lines or shapes.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:52.070
Do you guys visualize
anything in your heads

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:53.200
while you're playing?

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.190
- I can look at a lot of
different kinds of sheet music

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:56.850
and be able to hear it in my head,

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.490
which I think is one of
the interesting things

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.943
about reading music
versus not reading music.

00:08:00.943 --> 00:08:03.010
- When I'm playing I try
to pretend like I'm already

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:03.843
on the stage.

00:08:03.843 --> 00:08:07.400
Now, Nahre, for you that would
be visualizing 5,000 people

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:08.510
in a concert hall,
- No.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:09.343
solo piano.

00:08:09.343 --> 00:08:10.374
- No, no, no, no, no.

00:08:10.374 --> 00:08:11.350
(laughs)

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:12.258
- Black shawl.

00:08:12.258 --> 00:08:13.091
(laughs)

00:08:13.091 --> 00:08:14.447
Black shawl killin'.

00:08:14.447 --> 00:08:15.497
- Riotous applause.

00:08:15.497 --> 00:08:16.330
(laughs)

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:17.580
- Got to visualize the outfit, too.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:18.670
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:19.743
- In a prom dress.

00:08:19.743 --> 00:08:22.334
(laughs)

00:08:22.334 --> 00:08:24.351
(fast music)

00:08:24.351 --> 00:08:25.934
Yep, yep, yep, yep.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:27.783
Yeah.

00:08:39.391 --> 00:08:40.736
(laughs)

00:08:40.736 --> 00:08:41.771
- Come on.

00:08:41.771 --> 00:08:42.604
- That was a metronome, though.

00:08:42.604 --> 00:08:43.437
- No.

00:08:43.437 --> 00:08:44.630
That was a lot, that was a lot.

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:46.400
- We'd like to thank
Adam for jamming with us

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:47.720
